{ Welcome to my repertoire on the Grunfeld Defense! The Grunfeld is perhaps the sharpest response to 1.d4 and is a great option if you want a sharp tactical fight.

Before we begin, there are two warnings I must say about the Grunfeld. First, this opening is not suited for beginners. If you are a beginning player, you should stay away from this opening. Second, the opening is highly theoretical and memorization of opening lines is crucial if you want to find success with the Grunfeld. However, if you do learn the lines, you will be rewarded with an excellent fighting weapon to destroy 1. d4!

Finally, I won't cover any sidelines before move three. There's too much there to fit into one study and I'd rather focus on the main opening here. Make sure you do learn lines against the London, Jobava London, etc. } 1. d4 { White plays the queen's pawn opening. Note that White's move occupies the center with pawns. This gives us an excellent target to aim for. } 1... Nf6 { We don't want White to be able to play the move e2-e4, which would give White a great space advantage. } 2. c4 { White now fights for the d5-square and gains some space on the queenside. } 2... g6 { We prepare to put our bishop on the g7-square, where it puts pressure on White's pawn center. Attacking White's center is the main theme of the opening. } 3. Nc3 { White develops a knight and prepares to play e2-e4, laying claim to the whole center. } 3... d5 { This move stops White's plan of e2-e4 and gives Black a stake in the center. Here there are many options for White, which we will analyze in the following chapters. } *


