{ The first option we will look at for White is the Exchange Variation, which is the most critical line for White. } 4. cxd5 { This move is extremely logical. White trades a wing pawn for a center pawn, giving White a central majority. The game now becomes extremely lively. } 4... Nxd5 { Black recaptures the pawn. The knight is now solidly planted on the central d5-square, and White should not allow this. } 5. e4 { White attacks the knight on d5 and claims the center. Black must not allow White a free hand now.
If removing the knight was so important, why didn't White trade? } (5. Nxd5?! { is not a good move. } 5... Qxd5 { places the queen on an active square in the center of the board, and White doesn't have a good way of harassing it. Black has an easy game in this case. }) (5. Bd2 { is another line. After } 5... Bg7 6. e4 Nxc3 7. Bxc3 O-O { Black will continue to pressure White's center. A sample line is } 8. Qd2 c5 9. d5 e6 10. Bc4 Bxc3 11. bxc3 exd5 12. Bxd5 Nd7 { where Black is fine. }) 5... Nxc3 { Black captures White's knight. This move seems illogical, since White now gets to reinforce the d-pawn by recapturing. But let's see what happens if Black retreats the knight instead. } (5... Nb6?! 6. Be3 Bg7 7. h3! { (restricting Black's bishop to allow the knight onto f3) } 7... O-O 8. Nf3 { gives White a sizable advantage. Black doesn't have a good way to attack the d4-pawn. }) 6. bxc3 { White recaptures the knight. Black has two good moves here. } 6... Bg7 { Black places the bishop on the long diagonal, where it pressures White's d4-pawn. This move also prepares to castle. Black will look to play c7-c5, which puts a lot of pressure on White's center.
Now we reach a large splitting point in the theory of the Exchange Grunfeld. We will examine the three most common moves, which are Bc4, Nf3, and Be3. Each has been played thousands of times in master games. } { [%csl Gc5,Gc6,Gd4][%cal Gc7c5,Gb8c6,Gg7d4] } (6... c5 { is also possible here. There's nothing wrong with this approach to the Grunfeld, but it isn't the most testing move. }) *


