7. Bc4 { This move is the classical approach to the Grunfeld. White has a strong pawn center and figures they might as well develop their bishop to an active square. } 7... c5 { Black immediately targets White's d4-pawn, which forces White to make a decision about how they will defend it. } (7... O-O { is also possible here, but I find ...c5 to be more accurate. }) 8. Ne2 { White defends the pawn by putting the knight on the e2-square. Why does White do that instead of placing the knight on the active f3-square? } (8. Nf3 { is not necessarily a bad move, but it exposes White to a later Bg4, pinning the knight and taking away a defender of the d4-pawn. } 8... O-O 9. h3 { does avoid this problem, but White is forced to spend a valuable tempo to keep the bishop away. } { [%csl Gg4][%cal Gh3g4] } (9. O-O cxd4 10. cxd4 Nc6 11. Be3 Bg4 { is a bit uncomfortable for White. }) 9... Nc6 10. Be3 cxd4 11. cxd4 Qa5+ { gives Black a fine game. }) (8. Be3 { is also possible. After } 8... Nc6 9. Ne2 { we end up back in our main line. }) 8... Nc6 { Black continues to put pressure on the d4-pawn, which White is working hard to defend. Notice that all of Black's developed pieces, the c5-pawn, and the queen are all working together to attack this pawn. } 9. Be3 { White defends the pawn once again. Note that White's pieces haven't been able to go to their most active squares due to Black's strong pressure. } (9. d5 { is not a very good move. White is weakening the dark squares in the center, particularly the e5-square. This move also gives Black's bishop on g7 a good target to aim at. Black responds } { [%csl Ga1][%cal Gg7a1] } 9... Na5 { , harassing the bishop. } 10. Bb5+ (10. Bd3 O-O 11. O-O { is seen commonly at lower levels, but } 11... e6 { strikes back at White's center and gives Black a good game. }) 10... Bd7 11. Qa4 b6 { is fine for Black. }) 9... O-O { Black castles, getting the King to safety. Even in an opening such as the Grunfeld, it's still important to castle! } 10. O-O { White also castles. Both Kings have been tucked away on the kingside and we can turn our full attention to the battle for the center. } (10. Rc1 { is also possible here. After } 10... cxd4 11. cxd4 Qa5+ { , White must play } 12. Kf1 { to avoid losing the d4-pawn. After } 12... Qa3 { , Black has equalized the game. Two common lines are: } 13. Rc3 (13. Qb3 Qxb3 14. Bxb3 Rd8 15. d5 Na5 16. Ba4 b6) 13... Qd6 14. h4 h5 { shutting down White's kingside pawns. }) 10... Bd7 { Black develops the c8-bishop. This move is the favorite of the world's leading expert on the Grunfeld, Peter Svidler. } { [%csl Rb7] } (10... Bg4 { is the main line, provoking } 11. f3 { . Here Black chooses between Bg4-d7 and the more adventurous Na5. }) (10... Qc7 { is another popular line. After } 11. Rc1 { Black can play either b7-b6 or the more popular } 11... Rd8 { with strong pressure against d4. }) (10... b6 { is a very solid option, although Black doesn't have many winning chances in this line. }) 11. Rb1 { The drawback principle states that a player should always consider the negative side of the opponent's last move. Here, one such negative is that the b7-pawn is undefended. White brings their rook to the semi-open b-file and attacks the pawn that Black just weakened. } { [%csl Gb7][%cal Gb1b7] } 11... Qc7 { This move connects the rooks and defends the b7-pawn. Note that Black can now place a rook on d8, where it would pressure d4 once the bishop on d7 moves. Also, White's bishop on c4 is undefended. If the c-file were to open up, Black's queen would attack the bishop. } { [%cal Gc7c4,Gc5d4] } 12. Bd3 { This is White's most popular choice, retreating the bishop to get away from the queen on c7. } (12. Bf4 { is another move here. Black retreats with } 12... Qc8 { , leaving White with two options. } 13. d5 (13. Rc1 { is an indirect way to defend the bishop on c4. Once the c3-pawn recaptures on d4, the rook will be protecting the bishop. Black plays } 13... a6 14. Qd2 b5 15. Bd3 Qb7 16. Bh6 Qb6 17. Bxg7 Kxg7 { , achieving dynamic equality. }) 13... Na5 14. Bd3 e5 { forces the bishop to move. White's best retreat is } 15. Be3 { , after which Black plays } 15... f5 16. exf5 gxf5 { with a dynamic position. }) 12... Rad8 { Although either rook can move to d8, this is the more popular approach. Black places heavy pressure on the d4-pawn. } 13. Qc1 { This is the strongest option White has in this position. White reasons that one of Black's most powerful pieces is the bishop on g7, so they should trade it off for their own dark-squared bishop. } (13. f4 { looks scary but after } 13... Bg4 { White can't chase away the bishop with f2-f3, further weakening the d4-pawn. }) 13... Bc8 { Here is the advantage of moving the a8-rook to d8 instead of the f8-rook. Black's bishop can easily step aside, where it unleashes the full force of Black's rooks against the pawn on d4. } 14. Rd1 { White indirectly defends the pawn on d4 and also makes sure that the bishop on d3 isn't undefended. Notice how almost all of the moves by each side are trying to take control of the center. } 14... b6 { This move shores up the defense of the c5-pawn. Black can also redeploy the bishop on c8 to find a more active square for it. After this move, Black is doing fine. } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gc5d4,Gc6d4,Gg7d4,Gd8d4] } (14... cxd4? { would be a huge mistake. After } 15. cxd4 { Black cannot capture on d4 with the knight due to the pin from White's queen on c1. White has a massive advantage in this position. }) *


