7. Nf3 { This move seems illogical at first glance. White is exposing the knight to a pin by the c8-bishop, which otherwise wouldn't have much to do in this opening. However, White has a clever way of countering Black's pin. } 7... c5 { Black plays the typical Grunfeld move, striking at White's d4-pawn. Here there are two major options for White, as well as a third option that Black should be aware of. } (7... Bg4?! { is premature. The drawback principle helps us figure out what is wrong with this move. By moving the bishop, Black leaves the pawn on b7 without protection. White plays } 8. Qb3! { to harass the b7-pawn. This move gives White a small advantage. } { [%csl Gb7][%cal Gb3b7] }) *


[Event "Grunfeld Defense Repertoire: The Exchange Variation, 7. Nf3, 8. Rb1"]
[Date "2024.05.31"]
