[Opening "Grünfeld Defense: Exchange Variation, Modern Exchange Variation"]
[StudyName "Grunfeld Defense Repertoire"]
[ChapterName "The Exchange Variation, 7. Nf3, 8. Rb1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/HlS091Xs/cte7p13T"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/IndianDefense"]
[FEN "rnbqk2r/pp2ppbp/6p1/2p5/3PP3/2P2N2/P4PPP/R1BQKB1R w KQkq - 0 8"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2024.05.31"]
[UTCTime "12:39:23"]
[Orientation "black"]

8. Rb1 { This move is a very clever way of avoiding Black's pinning idea. Black cannot move the bishop because any such move would hang the b7-pawn. Aside from this, the move has its positional merit, too. White is placing a rook on a semi-open file, which is a very simple but effective idea in this position. } { [%csl Gb7,Rg4][%cal Gb1b7,Rc8g4] } 8... O-O { Black castles, getting the king to safety. } (8... Nc6?! 9. d5! Bxc3+ 10. Bd2 { is now possible for White since the rook isn't on a1 anymore. These minor nuances are what makes this opening so interesting. After } 10... Bxd2+ 11. Qxd2 { , White has a large advantage. }) 9. Be2 { This is the most logical move, preparing to castle Kingside. The bishop is well placed here since it doesn't get in the way of White's defense of d4. } (9. Bc4 { is not a good idea here. After } 9... Nc6 { White can either transpose into the 7. Bc4 line with the knight on f3 (which we saw was a bad idea) or play } 10. d5 Bxc3+ 11. Bd2 Bxd2+ 12. Qxd2 Na5 { where Black is doing quite well. }) 9... cxd4 { This move is certainly not the only move Black can play. Clarifying the central situation early on gives Black a simpler time than they would otherwise have. } (9... Nc6 10. d5 Ne5 11. Nxe5 Bxe5 12. Qd2 e6 { has been tried with decent success. Play continues } 13. f4 Bc7 14. O-O exd5 15. exd5 Ba5 { with an approximately equal position. }) (9... b6 { is also possible here. Usual is } 10. O-O Bb7 11. Qd3 Ba6 12. Qe3 Qd7 { with a slight advantage to White. However, Black has several other ideas in this variation that you can explore if you are interested. }) 10. cxd4 { White recaptures the pawn. White's pawn center is quite imposing; Black must react quickly if they are going to avoid getting slowly squeezed to death. } 10... Qa5+ { This move exploits one of the key drawbacks of 8. Rb1: it leaves the a2-pawn undefended. Black's queen is brought out early in order to take advantage of this. } { [%csl Ga2][%cal Ga5a2] } 11. Bd2 { Now we get to see one of the most fascinating variations in modern opening theory. } (11. Qd2 { is a move here, but it isn't very challenging. Black plays } 11... Qxd2+ 12. Bxd2 e6 { and achieves a completely equal game. A typical continuation would be } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Ge6d5] } 13. O-O b6 14. Rbc1 Bb7 15. Bb4 Rd8 16. Bb5 Ba6 17. a4 Bxb5 18. axb5 a6 { where Black creates an outside passed pawn for counterplay in the ending. }) 11... Qxa2 { Black completes their plan of capturing the a2-pawn. Now the game becomes very tactical and concrete. } 12. O-O { White castles. Here is a good time to take a good look at the position. What does White have in return for the pawn? The powerful pawn center stands out here. Also, White's strong rook on b1 is immobilizing the bishop on c8. So what does Black do? } 12... Bg4 { We move the bishop anyway! This move is very consistent with Black's ideas in the Grunfeld. Black isn't concerned about the b7-pawn because they want to put a lot of pressure on White's center.
Notice the recurring theme of aiming for piece activity, even if other concessions have to be made. } 13. Bg5 { This is the most common move for White. White wants to exchange dark-squared bishops via Bh6, but Black's queen was exerting annoying pressure on the position. This move, taking a piece away from the queen's line of sight, helps to relieve that pressure. } (13. Be3 { is also possible here, with similar ideas. Play continues } 13... Nc6 { and now there are two major options for White: } 14. d5 (14. Rxb7 Rab8 15. Rc7 Rfc8 16. Rxc8+ Rxc8 17. h3 Bxf3 18. Bxf3 Rd8 { gives Black play against the d4-pawn. White usually plays } 19. d5 Ne5 { , after which the position becomes similar to the above variation, with a pair of rooks being traded. } { [%csl Ga5][%cal Ga7a5] }) 14... Bxf3 15. Bxf3 Ne5 { , returning the pawn in order to blockade White's central majority. After } 16. Rxb7 a5 { Black will use the passed a-pawn for counterplay. } { [%cal Ga5a1] }) (13. Rxb7 Bxf3 14. Bxf3 Bxd4 { is certainly possible. White allows Black to remove the central d4-pawn, but gets a lot of activity in return. Here there are a few different lines: } 15. Bb4 { is a discovered attack against the bishop on d4, while also targeting the pawn on e7. } (15. e5 Na6 16. Rxe7 { is also an option. After } 16... Rad8 { the ending becomes pretty simple. }) 15... Rd8 { threatens Bxf2+, winning the queen. } 16. Qc1 { avoids this threat. } 16... Na6 { develops the knight and gives Black's a8-rook some room to breathe. Notice how the bishop on f3 hungrily eyed the rook, which was trapped before this move. } 17. Bxe7 Rac8 18. Qf4 Nc5 { continues the lively tactical play, with both sides maximizing the activity of their pieces. One very important detail here is that the queen on a2 defends the f7-pawn! } 19. Bxd8 (19. Bxc5 Rxc5 20. Bd1 { threatens Bd1-b3, winning due to threats against f7. Black plays } 20... Be5 { , after which the position is balanced. } 21. Qh4 Rf8 22. Bb3 Qa5 23. Qe7 Rc7 24. Rxc7 Qxc7 25. Qxc7 Bxc7 { is a drawn endgame. }) 19... Nxb7 20. Bf6 Bxf6 21. Qxf6 Nc5 { activates the knight. The position is even. }) 13... h6 { Black doesn't want the bishop to be posted on such an active square. This move forces a retreat because the pawn on e4 is undefended. } 14. Be3 { White retreats and covers the d4-pawn. } (14. Bxe7 Re8! { is Black's point. Moving the bishop would lose the e4-pawn, while } 15. Rxb7 Nd7 16. Bb4 Rxe4 { is quite comfortable for Black. }) (14. Bh4 { gives Black easy equality. } 14... g5 15. Bg3 Nc6 16. d5 Rad8 17. Rxb7 e6 { is the point. White can't maintain the central pawn wedge, and } 18. d6 Bxf3 19. Bxf3 Qa6 20. Qb1 Be5 21. d7 Bxg3 22. hxg3 Ne5 23. Rd1 Qc6 24. Rxa7 Rxd7 { is an even endgame. }) 14... Nc6 { Black develops the knight and steps up the pressure on the d4-pawn. } 15. d5 { White doesn't want to give up the pawn, so they must push it. } 15... Bxf3 { Black exchanges the light-squared bishop in order to gain access to the e5-square. } 16. Bxf3 { White makes the logical recapture. } (16. gxf3 { is actually a very good move in this position. The point is that Black's knight can't stay on e5 since White will kick it away with f3-f4. Now Black has a tactical trick that ensures a good game. } 16... Nd4! 17. Bd3 (17. Bxd4 Bxd4 18. Qxd4 Qxe2 { is fine for Black. }) 17... a5 18. f4 b5 19. Bxd4 Bxd4 20. Bxb5 Bc5 21. Qd3 Bb4 { is an equal endgame. }) 16... Ne5 { This is an excellent square for the knight, where it restricts White's center pawns and eyes squares inside White's territory. } 17. Rxb7 { White gets the pawn back. Now the rook is strongly posted on the seventh rank. } 17... a5 { Black sets their passed pawn in motion. } 18. Be2 { White makes sure that Black can't capture the bishop, maintaining the two-bishop advantage. } (18. Rxe7 a4 19. Bd4 Nxf3+ 20. gxf3 Bxd4 21. Qxd4 a3 { isn't a problem for Black. After } 22. d6 Qb2 23. Qd5 Qf6 24. e5 Qg5+ 25. Kh1 Qf5 { Black is doing well. }) (18. Bc5 { is also a possibility. Black plays } 18... a4 19. Bxe7 Rfb8 20. Rxb8+ Rxb8 21. d6 Qe6 22. Qxa4 Nxf3+ 23. gxf3 Qh3 24. Qa5 Qxf3 { with possibilities for a perpetual check. White can avoid this by } 25. Bh4 { but } 25... Qxe4 26. Bg3 Rb1 { is fine for Black. A few example lines: } 27. d7 (27. Qd2 Rxf1+ 28. Kxf1 Qh1+ 29. Ke2 Qe4+ 30. Kd1 Qf3+ 31. Kc2 Qe4+ 32. Qd3 Qxd3+ 33. Kxd3 Kf8 34. Ke4 Ke8 35. Kd5 Kd7 36. Be5 Bf8 { with a draw. The perpetual check idea can force White to trade queens, and the bishop endgame is drawn. }) (27. Qd8+ Kh7 28. Qe7 Rxf1+ 29. Kxf1 Qh1+ 30. Ke2 Qd5 31. d7 Qc4+ 32. Kf3 Qd5+ 33. Ke3 Qd4+ 34. Ke2 Qc4+ { etc. }) 27... Rxf1+ 28. Kxf1 Qd3+ 29. Kg2 Qxd7) 18... Nc4 { Black places the knight on an active square and threatens to take the bishop on e3. } 19. Bf4 { White sidesteps this threat. The bishop also will help to defend the passed pawn after White plays Rxe7 and d6. } (19. Rxe7 a4 { is better than taking the bishop. Here the position is about equal. }) 19... a4 { Black pushes the passed pawn. Defending the e7-pawn isn't much of a concern at this point. } 20. Rxe7 { White takes the pawn, creating a passed d-pawn. } 20... a3 { Advancing the pawn even further. } 21. Rc7 { White threatens to take the knight on c4. However, now that the rook has left the e-file, Black can move the knight there. } 21... Ne5 { This is an active post for the knight. By blocking the bishop's influence, Black is able to more easily defend against the d-pawn. } 22. d6 { White now wants to take on e5, followed by f2-f4 and e4-e5. } 22... Qb2 { Black asserts control of the long diagonal. If White isn't careful, Black will queen their own pawn. } 23. Rc2 { The only move. Other moves are much worse. For example: } (23. Qd2 g5 { [%cal Gg5f4] } 24. Qxb2 axb2 25. Bxe5 Bxe5 26. Rb7 Rfb8 { is much better for Black. } { [%csl Gb2][%cal Gb8b2] }) (23. Bxe5 Qxe5 24. f4 Qd4+ 25. Kh1 a2 { is winning for Black, utterly refuting White's idea. }) 23... Qb7 { Controlling the d7-square and preventing the pawn's further advance, while simultaneously attacking the e4-pawn. } 24. Ra2 { White hurries to get in front of the pawn before it becomes too dangerous. } 24... Qxe4 { Black captures the e4-pawn, restoring the material balance. After the further moves } 25. Bc1 Qb4 { The position is equal. } *


