[Orientation "black"]

8. Be3 { This logical move shores up the defense of the d4-pawn. White wants to maintain their powerful center and use it to squeeze Black with a space advantage. } 8... Qa5 { Black's policy in the Grunfeld is to make sure that every piece is doing something. Here Black goes after the c3-pawn with the queen. Because White already moved the bishop, Be3-d2 is a dubious defense. This move also clears the d8-square, where a rook would like to go later. } 9. Qd2 { This is the usual way of defending the c3-pawn. The bishop doesn't have to move again, and if Black's queen goes away, a later Be3-h6 might be possible, exchanging bishops. } (9. Nd2 { has also been played. Black can go for } 9... O-O (9... cxd4 10. Nc4 Qd8 11. cxd4 f5 { is good if you want to avoid the draw }) (9... Qxc3 10. Rc1 Qb4 11. Rxc5 O-O { is okay but not quite as good as the other options. }) 10. Nb3 (10. Be2 { keeps the game alive. Here you should play } 10... b6 11. O-O Ba6 { with good play. }) 10... Qxc3+ 11. Bd2 Qb2 12. Bc1 { (all other moves are much better for Black) } 12... Qc3+ 13. Bd2 { etc. with a draw. }) 9... Nc6 { This move develops the knight and adds pressure to the d4-pawn. Black is ready to play Bc8-g4 next, with a lot of pressure. } 10. Rb1 { This move is typical of this variation, trying to tie the bishop down to the b7-pawn. } (10. Rc1 { is also possible. The idea is to play d4-d5 now that the c3-pawn has been sufficiently protected. Black plays } 10... cxd4 11. cxd4 Qxd2+ 12. Kxd2 O-O { , reaching an endgame. White has two main options here. } 13. d5 { is the usual move. After } (13. Bb5 { is another possibility. Usual is } 13... f5 14. exf5 Bxf5 15. Bxc6 bxc6 16. Rxc6 Rab8 { where Black's two bishops and piece activity compensate for the pawn. }) 13... Rd8 14. Ke1 Na5 15. Bg5 Bd7 16. Bd3 f5 { Black is doing quite well. }) 10... a6 { This useful move not only keeps White off of b5, preventing the idea of Rb1-b5 picking up the c5-pawn. } 11. Rc1 { The rook switches to the c-file, which will soon be opened. By protecting the c3-pawn, this move makes d4-d5 possible. } 11... cxd4 { Black wants to clarify the central situation. White should not be allowed to advance in the center. } 12. cxd4 { White simply recaptures the pawn. } 12... Qxd2+ { Trading queens here ensures an even endgame for Black. } 13. Kxd2 { White recaptures with the king. In the endgame, it is important to activate your king, and this move brings it closer to the center of the board. } 13... e6 { This useful restricting move discourages d4-d5, ensuring that the knight will have a good square. It also prepares a later f7-f5 advance. } 14. Bd3 { White develops their last minor piece. } (14. h4 { is another move order. After } 14... O-O 15. Bd3 h6 { we reach our main line. }) 14... O-O { Black castles, allowing a rook to come to d8. } 15. h4 { This move gains kingside space and prepares to put a bishop on g5, keeping Black's rook off the d8-square. It also allows a later h4-h5 advance. } (15. Rc4 { defends the d4-pawn. Black plays } 15... Bd7 16. Rhc1 Rfd8 { with equality. }) 15... h6 { This move prevents White from placing a piece on d5. An h4-h5 advance can now be met with g6-g5, closing up the kingside. } 16. Rc5 { White moves the rook to a more active post and clears the way for the other rook to go to b1. } (16. e5 { can also be played. However, this move creates a backward d4-pawn and a hole on d5. Black plays } 16... Rd8 17. h5 g5 { trying to close the kingside. White's idea here is } 18. Nxg5 { trying to open up lines on the kingside. } 18... Nxd4! { allowing White to have the g5-pawn in exchange for the one on d4. } (18... hxg5 19. h6 { is crushing. }) 19. Ne4 (19. Bh7+ Kf8 20. Nxf7 Kxf7 21. Rc7+ Kf8 22. Rd1 Nf5+ 23. Ke2 Rxd1 24. Bc5+ Ke8 25. Kxd1 Bd7 { does not achieve anything for White. }) 19... Bxe5 20. Bxh6 f5 21. Nc5 Nc6 { is about equal. }) 16... Bd7 { Black develops their last minor piece and connects rooks. This move defends the knight on c6, allowing the b-pawn to move. } 17. Rb1 { White attacks the pawn on b7 and moves the rook to the semi-open b-file. } 17... b5 { Black advances to avoid the threat and activates their queenside majority. } 18. e5 { White also advances, blocking the g7-bishop's influence. } 18... Rfc8 { Black brings a rook to the open c-file to challenge White's control of the file. } 19. Be4 { White immobilizes the knight on c6 with a pin. } 19... Bf8 { Black evicts the rook from c5. The bishop has no future on the long diagonal, so moving it away has no real downside. } 20. Rcc1 { White retreats the rook. } 20... Rab8 { Black gets out of the pin and prepares to advance the b-pawn. } 21. Nh2 { White begins to reroute the knight to the weak f6-square. } 21... Be7 { Black covers that square and attacks the h4-pawn. } 22. Ng4 { White continues the knight maneuver. } 22... h5 { Black saves the h6-pawn and kicks the knight away. } 23. Nf6+ { It is better for White to exchange the knight than to retreat it. Other moves would lose the h4-pawn. } 23... Bxf6 { Black must take the knight, as the king and d7-bishop were forked. } 24. exf6 { White recaptures. } 24... e5 { Black goes after White's pawn on d4. } 25. d5 { White advances the pawn. } 25... Na5 { Black gets the knight to safety and prepares to place it on the strong c4-square. The position is equal. } *


