7. Be3 { This logical move defends the d4-pawn and keeps White's position flexible. The position could easily transpose into other lines. } 7... c5 { Black plays the typical pawn thrust, trying to destroy White's center. Here there are a few options for White. } 8. Qd2 { This is the most common move. White looks to play Be3-h6, exchanging bishops. The move also protects the c3-pawn. } (8. Rc1 { is also possible. I'd recommend } 8... Qa5 9. Qd2 cxd4 10. cxd4 Qxd2+ { , entering an endgame. We will look at this position in more depth later in the chapter, but for now I'll present two sample lines. } 11. Bxd2 (11. Kxd2 O-O 12. Nf3 Rd8 13. Bb5 Nc6 14. Bxc6 bxc6 15. Rc5 f5 { is another sample variation. Black is doing well. }) 11... O-O 12. Nf3 Bg4 13. Be3 Nc6 14. d5 Bxf3 15. gxf3 Nd4 { achieves equality. }) 8... Qa5 { This move prepares to capture on d4 and trade queens, achieving an equal endgame. } 9. Rc1 { White defends the c3-pawn. If Black exchanges on d4, the rook will suddenly be dominating the open c-file. } (9. Rb1 { attacks the b7-pawn. Black plays } 9... b6 10. Bb5+ Bd7 { , after which White has two main options. } 11. Be2 (11. Bd3 Nc6 12. Ne2 O-O 13. dxc5 Ne5 14. cxb6 axb6 { gives Black good compensation for the pawn. White can't play } 15. Bxb6 { because } 15... Qxa2 { gives Black a large advantage. }) 11... Bc6 12. Bd3 Nd7 13. Ne2 Rd8 { is good for Black. }) (9. Nf3 Nc6 { transposes to the 7. Nf3, 8. Be3 variation. }) 9... cxd4 { Black exchanges, heading for an even endgame. } 10. cxd4 { White recaptures on d4. } 10... Qxd2+ { Black trades queens, heading for an endgame. Black has a queenside majority of pawns, so they might be able to get an outside passed pawn. } 11. Bxd2 { Recapturing with the bishop is slightly more common than taking with the king. } (11. Kxd2 { is also possible. Here Black should play } 11... O-O { giving White three major options: } 12. Bb5 (12. Nf3 Rd8 13. Bb5 Nc6 14. Bxc6 bxc6 15. Rc5 f5 { achieves equality. }) (12. Bd3 Rd8 13. Ne2 Nc6 14. Rc4 Be6 { is equal as well. }) 12... Rd8 13. Ne2 Nc6 14. Bxc6 bxc6 { is fine for Black. } 15. Rxc6 Bb7 16. Rc7 Bxe4 17. f3 Bd5 18. Rxe7 Bf8 19. Rc7 Bxa2 { maintains the material balance and gives Black a good position with an outside passed a-pawn. }) 11... O-O { Black castles, tucking the king away. This move also defends the c8-bishop. } (11... Bxd4?? 12. Rxc8+ { is obviously not a good idea. }) 12. Nf3 { White now develops the knight and defends the d4-pawn. } 12... Bg4 { Black threatens to capture on f3 and win the d4-pawn. } (12... e6 { is also acceptable, for example } 13. Bb5 Nc6 14. Bxc6 bxc6 15. O-O Ba6 16. Rfe1 Bb5 { where Black is doing okay. }) 13. Be3 { This move defends the pawn on d4. } (13. d5 Nd7 { is very good for Black. The knight will become active and give Black a good position. }) 13... Nc6 { Increasing the pressure against d4 and developing the knight. White can no longer defend the pawn and must advance it. } (13... Rd8 { is another option. Typical would be } 14. Be2 e6 15. Ne5 Bxe2 16. Kxe2 f6 17. Nf3 Nc6 { which is okay for Black. }) (13... Bxf3 14. gxf3 { is yet another option and now } 14... e6 15. Bb5 a6 16. Ba4 b5 17. Bb3 a5 18. a4 bxa4 19. Bxa4 Na6 { is fine for Black. Any of these three moves will suffice for equality. }) 14. d5 { White advances the pawn, attacking the knight. } 14... Bxf3 { Throwing in this exchange gives Black a structural advantage. } 15. gxf3 { White recaptures. } 15... Nd4 { Bringing the knight to the active d4-square and attacking White's pawn on f3. } (15... Ne5? { allows } 16. f4 { , mobilizing the center with tempo. White has the advantage here. }) 16. Bh3 { This move prevents Black from challenging the c-file by covering the c8-square. } (16. Rc7 { is met with } 16... e6 17. dxe6 Nxe6 18. Rxb7 Rab8 19. Rxb8 Rxb8 { where Black can easily hold the ending due to White's damaged pawn structure. }) 16... f5 { Black strikes at the center and blocks White's bishop, allowing rooks onto the c8-square. } (16... Nxf3+?! 17. Ke2 Nd4+ 18. Kd3 { is good for White. The King becomes very active and White gets a dominant position. }) 17. Kf1 { White steps aside with the King. The idea is to get the king to g2, where it won't be in the way of the rook on h1. } 17... Nxf3 { Black takes the f3-pawn. } 18. exf5 { Now that Black can't recapture with the knight, this exchange works well. } 18... gxf5 { Recapturing the pawn. } 19. Rc7 { White infiltrates with the rook, giving White an active position. } 19... Rad8 { Black counterattacks the d5-pawn. } 20. Rxe7 { White captures the pawn on e7. } 20... Kh8 { Stepping out of any pins on the g-file before they cause any trouble. The position is balanced. } *


