5. Qb3 { This move, known as the Russian Variation, is an extremely complex line. White brings out the queen to pressure the d5-pawn, forcing Black to capture on c4. } 5... dxc4 { Black is forced to capture due to the threat against the d5-pawn. } (5... c6 6. cxd5 cxd5 7. Bg5 e6 8. e3 O-O 9. Bd3 { favors White. }) 6. Qxc4 { White recaptures the pawn, but the queen is exposed and can become a tactical target. } 6... O-O { Black immediately castles, bringing the King to safety. } 7. e4 { White takes over the center. The e4-d4 pawn duo should not be taken lightly and Black can get completely steamrolled if they are not careful. } (7. Bf4 { is another option, targeting the c7-pawn. } 7... c6 8. e4 b5 9. Qb3 Qa5 10. Bd3 Be6 { is satisfactory for Black. }) 7... a6 { This move introduces the Hungarian Variation. Black covers the b5-square and is ready to play b7-b5 and activate their pieces. Black will quickly launch their queenside pawns forward and get good play. In this variation, White has several major options. } *


