8. Be2 { This solid move develops the bishop and prepares to castle. White isn't interested in anything aggressive; they trust their center and space advantage to give them good chances. They try to consolidate and receive a small positional edge. } 8... b5 { Black immediately gains queenside space and harasses White's advanced queen. } 9. Qb3 { White gets the queen out of danger. } (9. Qd3 { has also been played, notably by Carlsen. Black plays } 9... c5 10. dxc5 Qxd3 11. Bxd3 Bb7 12. Be3 Ng4 { with sufficient compensation for the pawn. Black is doing quite well here. }) 9... c5 { Black immediately breaks up White's center. } 10. dxc5 { White captures the c5-pawn. } (10. d5? e6 { gives Black an advantage. }) 10... Be6 { This move takes advantage of White's exposed queen and develops a bishop with tempo. } 11. Qc2 { White retreats the queen. } 11... Nbd7 { Black develops the knight and aims to recapture the c5-pawn. } 12. Be3 { White develops the bishop. White does not want to allow Black to take on c5 since that would give Black an easy game. } 12... Rc8 { Black insists on capturing the pawn on c5. The battle over the c5-pawn will continue to rage for a few more moves. } 13. Rd1 { White pins the knight to the queen, preventing Black from capturing the pawn. } (13. b4?! Nxe4! { is very good for Black. }) 13... b4 { This move is very difficult to face as White. White is forced into either breaking the pin or placing the knight offside on a4. } 14. Nd5 { White usually plays this move, placing the knight in the center of the board but breaking the pin against the d7-knight. } (14. Na4 { is also possible, but after } 14... Qa5 15. O-O Nxe4 16. Qxe4 Qxa4 17. Ng5 Nxc5 { Black is doing well. }) 14... Bxd5 { White's knight on d5 was stronger than Black's e6-bishop, so Black trades. } 15. exd5 { White recaptures on d5. } 15... Nxc5 { Black gets the pawn back. } 16. O-O { White brings their King to safety and connects rooks. } (16. Bxc5?! Nd7 { wins the piece back with a slight advantage. }) 16... Nce4 { Black discovers an attack on the queen and places their knight on a strong central square. This isn't the only move Black can play, however. } (16... a5 { is about as popular as ...Nce4. This move has been played by many strong players as well. }) (16... Qd6 { is also possible here, and is only slightly less common than the other two moves. }) 17. Qd3 (17. Qb1 Nc5 { is another line here. Typical is } 18. Bc4 Qc7 19. Bd4 Rfd8 { which is acceptable for Black. }) 17... Qd6 { Black moves the queen to a better square and defends the b4-pawn. } 18. Qxa6 { White takes the pawn on a6, temporarily gaining a material advantage. } (18. g3 { is another line, preparing Bf4 to hit the queen. Usual is } 18... Rfd8 19. Bf4 Qxd5 20. Qxa6 Qa8 21. Qxa8 Rxa8 22. Rxd8+ Rxd8 { with a decent endgame for Black. }) 18... Rc2 { Black counterattacks the b2-pawn. } (18... Qxa6 19. Bxa6 Rc2 20. Bd4 Nd6 { is also fine for Black. }) 19. Bd4 { White centralizes the bishop and defends the b2-pawn. } 19... Nc5 { Black moves the knight back to force White to trade queens. } 20. Qxd6 { White trades. } (20. Qb5 Rb8 21. Qc6 Nxd5 22. Qxd6 exd6 { would be fine for Black. }) 20... exd6 { Black recaptures. The knight on c5 is now firmly planted. } 21. Bb5 { White gets the bishop to safety and places it on an active square. } 21... Nfe4 { Black advances their knight and offers an exchange of bishops. } 22. Rc1 { White trades rooks to avoid losing the b2-pawn. } 22... Rxc1 { Black has no choice. They must exchange rooks. } 23. Rxc1 { White recaptures. } 23... Ra8 { Black brings the rook to the semi-open a-file and targets the pawn on a2. } 24. Bxg7 { White exchanges bishops. Other bishop moves would have lost the b2-pawn. } 24... Kxg7 { Black recaptures. Despite being a pawn down, Black's excellent piece activity offers great chances to hold the ending. } *


