8. e5 { This sharp move immediately kicks away Black's knight at the cost of weakening the d5-square. } 8... Nfd7 { This move is not the main line, but I find it to be quite satisfactory for Black. } 9. Be3 { White develops their dark-squared bishop. } (9. e6 { is an interesting approach to the position. Black plays } 9... fxe6 10. Qxe6+ (10. Be3 Nb6 11. Qb3 a5 { is also fine for Black }) 10... Kh8 { and receives a good position. }) (9. Be2 { is another approach. After } 9... b5 10. Qd5 Nb6 11. Qe4 Bf5 12. Qh4 Nc6 { Black is doing okay. }) 9... b5 { Black harasses White's queen, trying to make White retreat. } 10. Qb3 { White retreats the queen. } (10. Qd5 Nb6 11. Qe4 Bf5 12. Qh4 f6 { is okay for Black. Usual is } 13. g4 Be6 14. Bg2 { and now Black equalizes with } 14... b4 15. Ne4 Bd5 { . Black has a great position. }) 10... c5 { With this pawn break, Black gets great piece play. } 11. e6 { White tries to get Black to take the e6-pawn, which would be great for White. } (11. dxc5 Nxe5 12. Nxe5 Bxe5 13. Rd1 Qa5 { is slightly better for Black. }) 11... cxd4! { Now some concrete variations arise. } 12. Nxd4 { White recaptures the pawn. } (12. exf7+ Rxf7 13. Nxd4 Nc5 14. Qd5 Qxd5 15. Nxd5 Nbd7 { is quite nice for Black. }) (12. Bxd4 Bxd4 13. Nxd4 Nc5 14. exf7+ Rxf7 15. Qd5 Qxd5 16. Nxd5 Nbd7 { is the above position with the dark-squared bishops removed. Similarly, Black is doing well here. }) (12. exd7? Qxd7 13. Rd1 e5 { is very good for Black. }) 12... Nc5 { Black simultaneously hits the queen and the e6-pawn. } 13. exf7+ { Forced, as other moves would lose material. } 13... Rxf7 { Black recaptures. } 14. Qd5 { White brings the queen to the center and offers a queen trade. } 14... Qxd5 { Black agrees to the trade, knowing the resulting endgame is quite nice for them. } 15. Nxd5 { White recaptures. } 15... Nbd7 { Black develops the knight and has a good position. } *


