8. Qb3 { This move anticipates Black's idea of b7-b5 and gets the queen out of harm's way. } 8... c5 { Black plays this move in order to break up White's center. } 9. dxc5 { White takes the pawn. } 9... Qa5 { Black wants to recapture the pawn. This move also pins the knight on c3, threatening to take on e4. } 10. Qb6 { White has only one good way to meet both threats. } 10... Qxb6 { Black trades queens. } 11. cxb6 { White recaptures. } 11... Nbd7 { Black develops the knight and will retake the b6-pawn, achieving a good game. } 12. Be3 { This isn't the most common move, but it does try to hold on to the pawn, so it's worth knowing how to respond. On other moves Black replies ...Nxb6 and has a good position. } 12... Ng4 { Immediately going after the bishop. } 13. Bg5 { White targets the e7-pawn. } 13... Nxb6 { Black takes the b6-pawn back, restoring the material balance. } 14. Bxe7 { White takes the e7-pawn. Now Black wins the e4-pawn with } 14... Bxc3+ 15. bxc3 Re8 { Black is doing very well. } *


