8. Qd2 { White wants to protect the knight instead of retreating it, reasoning that the knight stands better on g5 than it does on f3. } 8... exd5 { Black recaptures the pawn on d5, restoring the material balance. } 9. Qe3+ { This check takes advantage of the advanced knight and the fact that the queen cannot stop protecting the d5-pawn. Black must forfeit castling rights. } 9... Kf8 { This is the only good way to get out of the check. } (9... Be6 10. Nxe6 fxe6 11. Qxe6+ { wins for White. }) (9... Qe7 10. Qxe7+ Kxe7 11. Nxd5+ { is also winning for White. }) 10. Qf4 { Threatening mate on f7 and repositioning the queen to a better square. } 10... Bf6 { Black blocks the checkmate threat and threatens to take the knight on g5. } 11. h4 { White tries to maintain the powerful knight on g5. } 11... h6 { Black wisely evicts the knight. } 12. Nf3 { White retreats the knight. } (12. Nxd5 Bxg5 13. Qe5 Rh7 14. hxg5 Nc6 15. Qe4 Bf5 16. Qf3 Nxd4 17. Qa3+ Kg7 18. Ne3 Qxg5 { is fine for Black. Usual is } 19. Qc3 Qf6 20. O-O-O Ne6 { with an equal position. }) 12... Kg7 { Black has now finished "castling by hand" and allowed the rook on h8 to get into the game. } 13. O-O-O { White gets the king to safety. } (13. e3 { is another logical move. Black plays } 13... Be6 14. Bd3 c6 { with a balanced game. }) 13... Be6 { Black develops the bishop and reinforces the d5-pawn. } 14. e3 { White opens up the diagonal so the f1-bishop can be developed. } (14. e4 { is another typical move. Black plays } 14... dxe4 15. Nxe4 Bxa2 { with a good position. }) 14... Nd7 { Black has finished development and has a good position. } *


