6. Bh4 { This variation doesn't allow Black to gain the two-bishop advantage. } 6... Nxc3 { Black trades on c3, following the principle that the player with less space should trade pieces. } 7. bxc3 { White recaptures. } 7... dxc4 { Black wants to fix the pawn on c3, where a later c7-c5 might allow it to become a target. } 8. Qa4+ { White immediately gets the pawn back. } (8. e3 b5 9. a4 c6 { has also been tried here. White typically plays } 10. Nd2 (10. Be2 a6 11. Nd2 { transposes }) 10... a6 11. Be2 O-O 12. Bf3 Ra7 13. O-O Qc7 { with a small advantage. }) 8... c6 { Black blocks the check. and opens up a diagonal for their queen. } 9. Qxc4 { White gets the pawn back. } 9... Qa5 { Black brings the queen out to a5, where it plays an active role in the game. } 10. e3 { White solidifies the d4-pawn and opens up room for the bishop on f1 to move. } 10... Be6 { Black develops with tempo. } 11. Qb4 { White offers a trade of queens. } (11. Qd3 Nd7 { has also been played. This doesn't pose any problems for Black. }) 11... Qxb4 { Black accepts the trade. } 12. cxb4 { Recapturing. } 12... Nd7 { Black has finished development and reached a balanced position. } *


