6. Be2 { This solid move prepares castling. Often, players going for this variation will try to play simple, solid moves, so if we can cause complications, they will be out of their comfort zone. } 6... dxc4 { By capturing on c4, we force the bishop to move again. } 7. Bxc4 (7. O-O c5 8. dxc5 (8. Bxc4 { transposes to the main line }) 8... Qa5 { ensures good play for Black. Usual is } 9. Bxc4 (9. Qd4 Nbd7 10. Qxc4 Qxc5 11. Qh4 h6 { is another line. Black has no problems here. }) 9... Qxc5 { , which is fine for Black. }) 7... c5 { We immediately try to target the pawn on d4. } 8. O-O { White gets the King to safety. } (8. d5 { has been tried as well, but it isn't the most challenging move. Black plays } 8... e6 9. dxe6 (9. e4 exd5 10. exd5 Re8+ 11. Be3 Ng4 { is good for Black. }) 9... Qxd1+ 10. Kxd1 Bxe6 11. Bxe6 fxe6 { with a fine endgame for Black. }) 8... cxd4 { We want to create a target on d4. } 9. exd4 { White recaptures, creating an isolani position. } (9. Nxd4 a6 10. a4 Qc7 { is nice for Black. Our dark-squared bishop is much better than White's. }) 9... Bg4 { The way to beat an isolani is to exchange minor pieces, so Black immediately pounces on the knight on f3. } 10. Be3 (10. h3 Bxf3 11. Qxf3 Nc6 { is fine for Black. }) (10. d5 Nbd7 11. Re1 Rc8 12. Bb3 Nc5 { shouldn't pose any problems for us. }) 10... Nc6 { Developing the knight and pressuring the d4-pawn. } 11. d5 { White advances the pawn, attempting to prove that the isolated queen's pawn is strong here. } 11... Bxf3 { We exchange bishop for knight, knowing we will get White's light-squared bishop as well. } 12. Qxf3 { White recaptures. } 12... Ne5 { Forking the queen and light-squared bishop. } 13. Qe2 { This is the only way to avoid losing material. } 13... Nxc4 { We've eliminated White's bishop pair. } 14. Qxc4 { Recapturing. } 14... Qd7 { We get our queen out and connect rooks. We are ready to put our rooks to the open files and reach a good position.
Black has equalized the game here. } *


