6. cxd5 { We saw in the previous chapter how allowing ...dxc4 works out nicely for Black. This exchange allows White to develop the light-squared bishop without loss of time. } 6... Nxd5 { We recapture the pawn. } 7. Bc4 { One more benefit of this variation is that it allow White to post the bishop on a much more active square than e2. } 7... Nxc3 { Exchanging knights leads to satisfactory positions for Black. } 8. bxc3 { White recaptures. } 8... c5 { Immediately striking at the center. The position is similar to that of an Exchange Grunfeld, but with White's e-pawn less advanced. } 9. O-O { White castles, getting the King to safety. } 9... Qc7 { Trying to exploit the undefended position of the c4-bishop. } 10. Qe2 { This is the usual way for White to defend the bishop. } (10. Be2 b6 { leads to positions that are fine for Black. }) 10... Nc6 { Developing the knight and piling on pressure to the d4-pawn. } 11. Ba3 { This is the only active square for White's bishop to develop to. } (11. Rd1 { has also been played. After } 11... Rd8 12. h3 b6 { Black is fine. }) 11... b6 { Defending the c5-pawn. } (11... Na5 { is another option here. }) 12. Rac1 { Defending the c3-pawn and placing the rook opposite Black's queen. } (12. dxc5 bxc5 13. Bxc5 Ne5 14. Bd5 Rb8 { is fine for Black. }) 12... Bb7 { Black develops the bishop and connects rooks. } 13. Rfd1 { Bringing the other rook into play. } 13... Na5 { Black harasses the light-squared bishop. } 14. Bd3 { White retreats the bishop. } (14. Ba6 Bxa6 15. Qxa6 Rac8 { is also possible. Black is fine in this position. }) 14... Rfd8 { Black brings the rook into play. The position is about equal. } *


