6. Qb3 { White brings the queen out early to put pressure on the d5-pawn. } 6... e6 { Black reinforces the pawn and invites an exchange, which would be very favorable for them. This move is known as the Botvinnik Variation. } 7. Bd2 { This solid move develops the bishop to a decent square. White doesn't want to commit the light-squared bishop quite yet. } (7. Be2 { has also been played. I recommend } 7... b6 { giving White two options. } 8. O-O (8. cxd5 exd5 9. O-O Bb7 10. Rd1 Nbd7 11. Bd2 Re8 { is fine for Black. }) 8... Bb7 9. Rd1 Nbd7 10. Bd2 c5 { is nice for Black. }) 7... Nc6 { This move is a bit unusual, but it's fine for Black. } 8. cxd5 { White trades so that they can develop the light-squared bishop effectively. } 8... exd5 { Black recaptures with the pawn, maintaining a strong central foothold. } 9. Be2 { White brings the bishop out and prepares to castle. } 9... Ne7 { Black reroutes the knight to unblock the c-pawn. } 10. O-O { White castles, bringing the King to safety. } 10... c6 { Black reinforces the pawn on d5. The game is equal. } *


