6. b4 { This move, the Makogonov Variation, aims to rapidly gain queenside space. } 6... c6 { Black simply reinforces the d5-pawn. } 7. Bb2 { White develops the dark-squared bishop. } 7... Bg4 { Bringing the bishop out to a good square. We intend to exchange it for the f3-knight. } 8. h3 (8. Qb3 { has also been played. Black plays } 8... Bxf3 9. gxf3 e6 { and has a good position. }) 8... Bxf3 { Trading our bad bishop for White's knight. } 9. Qxf3 { White recaptures. } 9... a5 { We immediately go after White's extended queenside. } 10. b5 { White advances the pawn. If they are allowed to play a2-a4, the resulting structure will be heavily in White's favor. } 10... a4! { Stopping White's idea of a2-a4. With this move Black secures a good position. } *


