6. Bd2 { This move, the Opocensky Variation, simply develops the bishop and clears c1 for White's rook. } 6... c5 { Black immediately strikes in the center. } 7. dxc5 { This is by far the most challenging move. } (7. cxd5 cxd4 8. Nxd4 (8. exd4 Nxd5 { is an isolani position that favors Black }) 8... Nxd5 { gives Black an easy game. }) 7... Na6 { Black wants to recapture on c5 with the knight. } 8. cxd5 { White decides that it is the correct time to take the d5-pawn. } (8. Na4 { tries to hold on to the c5-pawn. Black plays } 8... Ne4 { with a superior position. }) 8... Nxc5 { Black recaptures the c5-pawn. } 9. Bc4 { This is the only move that lets White try for an edge. } (9. Be2 Nxd5 { is very nice for Black. }) 9... a6 { Black wants to play b7-b5, chasing the bishop away. If Black can retake the d5-pawn, then they will have a good position. } 10. a4 { White makes an attempt to prevent this from happening. } (10. b4 Nce4 11. Rc1 (11. Nxe4 Nxe4 12. Rc1 b5 13. Bb3 Bb7 { wins the pawn back with advantage }) 11... b5 12. Bb3 Bb7 13. O-O Qd6 { is fine for Black. }) 10... Bf5 { Black brings the bishop to an active diagonal and seizes control over the e4-square. } 11. O-O { White tucks the king away and brings the h1-rook into the game. } 11... Rc8 { Black brings the rook to the open c-file. } 12. Nd4 { White doesn't want Black's bishop to sit comfortably on f5. } (12. Qe2 { has also been played. After } 12... Nfe4 { Black has a comfortable position. }) 12... Bd3 { Black forces an exchange of bishops. } 13. Bxd3 { White has to take, otherwise the rook on f1 is lost. } 13... Nxd3 { Recapturing. Black is doing well here. } *


