[UTCTime "12:39:23"]
[Orientation "black"]

6. Rc1 { This move brings the rook to the c-file, which White could open at any time with cxd5. } 6... Be6 { This slightly uncommon move is the favorite of Maxime Vachier Lagrave. Both engines have it as the second choice behind the main move ...dxc4. Black decides that it is a good idea to target the pawn on c4. } 7. e3 { White opens up the diagonal for the bishop on f1. } (7. Ng5 c5 8. Nxe6 fxe6 9. e3 Nh5 { might look ugly, but it is actually quite nice for Black. }) 7... dxc4 { Black takes the c4-pawn. } 8. Ng5 { This move tries to exploit the position of the bishop on e6. } 8... Bd5 { Black moves the bishop away from the knight on g5. } 9. e4 { Claiming the center. } (9. Nxd5 Nxd5 10. Bxc4 Nxf4 11. Qf3 e6 12. Qxf4 c5 { is fine for Black. }) 9... h6! { It's very important not to move the bishop. } 10. exd5 { White takes on d5. } 10... hxg5 { Black takes the knight on g5. } 11. Bxg5 { Taking the pawn. } 11... Nxd5 { Black also takes on d5. } 12. Bxc4 { White restores the material balance and develops their bishop. } 12... Nb6 { Retreating the knight and attacking the bishop on c4. } 13. Bb3 { Retreating the bishop to its best square. } 13... Nc6 { Developing a knight and putting pressure on the d4-pawn. } 14. d5 { This move, advancing the isolani, is White's most popular option. } (14. Ne2 { has also been tried. } 14... a5 15. a4 Rc8 { (to guard the c7-pawn) } 16. O-O Nxd4 17. Nxd4 Qxd4 { is fine for Black. }) 14... Nd4 { Black gets the knight to safety. } 15. O-O { White brings their king out of the middle of the board. } 15... Qd7 { This move connects the rooks and brings the queen out. The position is balanced. } *


