{ By now we've covered all of the main lines, but there are a few more lines that White can play. } 4. Bf4 { This solid approach is called the Brinckmann Attack. White develops the bishop and will play e2-e3 next. } 4... Bg7 { Completing our fianchetto. } 5. e3 (5. Nf3 O-O { transposes to the Three Knights Variation, 5. Bf4 line. }) (5. Rc1 { has also been tried. Continuing with } 5... Nh5 6. Bg5 (6. Bd2 c5 { is also fine for Black }) 6... h6 7. Bh4?! (7. Bd2 { is necessary, but it's hardly ever played. } 7... dxc4 8. Nf3 O-O { is fine for Black. }) 7... c5 8. e3 cxd4 9. exd4 (9. Nxd5 Nc6 10. exd4 Be6 { is great for Black }) 9... Nc6 { gives Black the advantage. }) 5... O-O { This is the Grunfeld Gambit. Now White has two main options: Rc1 and cxd5.
6. Nf3 transposes to what we saw in the Three Knights Variation. } 6. Qb3 (6. Qb3 c5 7. cxd5 cxd4 8. exd4 Nbd7 9. Be2 Nb6 10. Bf3 Bf5 { gives Black enough compensation for the pawn. }) 6... c5 7. cxd5 cxd4 8. exd4 Nbd7 9. Be2 Nb6 10. Bf3 Bf5 { gives Black enough compensation for the pawn. } *


