6. Rc1 { This move places the rook on the c1-square, where it defends the c4-pawn and eyes the c7-pawn. } 6... dxc4 { This simple move achieves the typical Grunfeld structure. Black will put pressure on the d4-pawn. } 7. Bxc4 { White recaptures the pawn. } 7... Nbd7 { It's best not to rush with ...c5 since White will get good play from d5 or dxc5. } 8. Nf3 { White develops the knight and prepares to castle. } 8... c5 { Black strikes at White's center. White now must decide how to handle the central tension. } 9. dxc5 { This move is perhaps the simplest. The pawns are exchanged, leaving a symmetrical structure. } (9. O-O cxd4 10. exd4 { is also possible. Black plays to control the d5-square, fixing the d4-pawn as a permanent weakness. } 10... Nb6 11. Bb3 Nbd5 { gives approximately equal chances. }) 9... Nxc5 { Black recaptures the pawn. } 10. O-O { Here neither side wants to trade queens because doing so would give the opponent control of the d-file. } 10... Be6 { Exchanging light-squared bishops here neutralizes any pressure that White might dream of having. } 11. Bxe6 { White doesn't have any better options. } 11... Nxe6 { Recapturing and hitting the bishop on f4. } 12. Be5 { White moves the bishop onto the long diagonal. } (12. Bg3 Qb6 { would give Black a fine game. }) 12... Qa5 { Black activates the queen and prepares to bring rooks to the open files. The position is equal. } *


