6. cxd5 { With this move, White accepts the gambit. } 6... Nxd5 { Black recaptures, opening up the g7-bishop's diagonal. } 7. Nxd5 { This move draws Black's queen away from the protection of c7. } 7... Qxd5 { Black recaptures but leaves c7 undefended. } 8. Bxc7 { White takes the pawn and leaves it up to Black to prove that they have compensation. } 8... Na6 { This move attacks the bishop on c7 and clears the way for further development. } 9. Bxa6 { This is certainly the best move, ruining the queenside structure and interrupting Black's quick development. } (9. Bg3 Bf5 { gives Black very easy development and a tiny edge. }) 9... Qxg2 { This move recaptures the pawn. White cannot save both the rook and the bishop. } 10. Qf3 { This is the only move that saves the rook on h1. } 10... Qxf3 { Black now must exchange in order to win the piece back. } 11. Nxf3 { White recaptures and finishes the development of their minor pieces. } 11... bxa6 { Black wins the piece back. Here is a good time to take stock of what has happened.
Black has doubled a-pawns, although they do have only two pawn islands compared to White's three. Black also has the two bishops. White has a central majority which could eventually create a passed pawn, although advancing the pawns will be difficult due to Black's active bishops. } 12. Rc1 { White brings the rook to the open file and defends the bishop on c7. } (12. Rg1 { has also been tried. Black plays } 12... Be6 13. b3 Rfc8 14. Rc1 a5 15. Ke2 a4 16. bxa4 Bxa2 { with a fine position. }) 12... Bb7 { Black places the bishop on the extremely powerful a8-h1 diagonal and threatens to take White's knight on f3. } 13. Ke2 { White defends the knight and brings the king toward the center. } 13... Rac8 { Black challenges for control of the c-file. The bishop is good on c7, so Black wants to cause it some problems. The position is okay for Black. } *


