4. Bg5 { This move is similar to the Three Knights Variation, 5. Bg5 line. White wants to destabilize the d5-pawn. } 4... c5 { This is my favorite line! Dubov has played this with good success, too. } 5. Bxf6 { This is the best option for White, going after the pawn. } (5. e3 { is common online but isn't challenging. } 5... Bg7 6. Nf3 cxd4 7. Nxd4 Ne4 { gives Black a good position. }) (5. Nf3 cxd4 6. Nxd4 Bg7 { isn't a problem for Black either. }) 5... exf6 { Black recaptures. } 6. cxd5 { White takes the pawn. } (6. Nxd5 cxd4 7. Qxd4 Bb4+ 8. Kd1 Be7 { gives Black good compensation for the pawn. }) 6... Qb6 { This is the key move, exploiting the weak b2-pawn. } 7. e3 { White defends d4. } (7. Nf3 Qxb2 8. Qc1 Qxc1+ 9. Rxc1 cxd4 10. Nxd4 Nd7 { is equal. Black has the two bishops to compensate for the d5-pawn. }) (7. dxc5 Qxb2 8. Qc1 Qxc1+ 9. Rxc1 Bxc5 { is slightly better for Black. }) 7... cxd4 { Exchanging to weaken White's pawns further. } 8. Qxd4 { White recaptures with the queen to prevent ...Qxb2. } (8. Bb5+ Nd7 9. Qxd4 Qxd4 10. exd4 Be7 { is fine for Black. }) 8... Qxd4 { Black exchanges, ruining White's structure. } 9. exd4 { White recaptures. } 9... Bg7 { Developing the bishop. Black has a fine position. } *


