5. Qb3 { This move pressures the d5-pawn and is similar to the Russian Variation of the Three Knights. } 5... e6 { Black reinforces the pawn. } 6. Qa3 { White tries to stop Black from castling. } (6. Nf3 Nc6 (6... O-O { would reach by transposition the Botvinnik Variation, which is quite complex. If you want to learn this line, it is certainly fine for Black. }) 7. cxd5 exd5 8. Bb5 O-O 9. Bxc6 bxc6 { is fine for Black. }) 6... Nc6 { Black develops the knight and prepares to bring it to e7 so they can castle. } 7. Nf3 { White develops their own knight. } 7... Ne7 { Black blocks the queen out and prepares to castle. } 8. Be2 { White develops the bishop and prepares to castle themselves. } 8... O-O { Black castles. } 9. O-O { White also castles. } 9... b6 { Black prepares to fianchetto the bishop like in a Queen's Gambit Declined. The position is fine for Black. } *


