5. cxd5 { This is similar to an Exchange Variation, but with the pawn committed to e3. } 5... Nxd5 { Black takes the pawn back. The knight is quite strong on d5 so White must decide what to do about it. } 6. Nxd5 { White decides to trade off the knight. } (6. Bc4 { has also been played. } 6... Nxc3 7. bxc3 c5 8. Nf3 Qc7 9. Qe2 Nc6 10. O-O O-O { is fine for Black. }) 6... Qxd5 { Black recaptures. } 7. Ne2 { White develops the knight and prepares to bring it to c3. } (7. Nf3 { is not the correct square for the knight. } 7... c5 { puts the center under a lot of pressure and the bishop can come to g4 and exchange itself for the knight. }) 7... O-O { Black gets the King to safety and allows a rook to be placed on the d-file. } 8. Nc3 { White completes the knight maneuver and attacks Black's queen. } 8... Qd6 { Black gets the queen out of danger. } 9. Be2 { White develops the bishop and prepares to castle. } 9... c6 { Black blocks the long diagonal, anticipating that White will move their bishop to f3. } 10. O-O { White castles and gets the king out of the center. } 10... Nd7 { Black develops the knight. The position is equal. } *


