1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. h4 { This move must be given the respect it deserves. White doesn't let us get our normal Grunfeld setup by delaying development of the knight on b1. } 3... c5 { Black goes after the pawn on d4 immediately. White has "wasted" a move with h4, so we try to destroy White's center. } 4. d5 { White tries to achieve a central clamp. } 4... b5 { This Benko Gambit-type sacrifice gives Black excellent play. } 5. cxb5 { White can't really decline the pawn because the move h2-h4 would be severely weakening in those lines. } (5. h5 { has been tried, but } 5... Nxh5 6. Rxh5 gxh5 7. e4 d6 8. Qxh5 Nd7 { heading for f6 is fine for Black. }) 5... a6 { This usual move aims to get the light-squared bishop very active. } 6. e3 { White defends the pawn with the bishop on f1. } (6. bxa6 Bxa6 7. Nc3 Bg7 { is fine for Black. }) 6... Bg7 { Black completes the fianchetto. } 7. Nc3 { White develops a knight and adds another defender to b5. } 7... axb5 { This is the right time to exchange. } 8. Bxb5 { White recaptures. } 8... Ba6 { This trade of bishops gives Black queenside targets and a fine position. } *


