[FEN "rnbqkb1r/pp2pp1p/2p2np1/3p4/2PP4/6P1/PP2PPBP/RNBQK1NR w KQkq - 0 5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2024.05.31"]
[UTCTime "12:39:23"]
[Orientation "black"]

5. Qa4 { This isn't really dangerous, as the d5-pawn is well defended. } 5... Bg7 { We can safely ignore the queen and pressure d4. } 6. cxd5 Nxd5 7. Nf3 { The d4-pawn must be defended. } (7. e4 Nb6 8. Qd1 Qxd4 { is already great for Black and White has no compensation whatsoever for the pawn. }) 7... Nb6 { Forcing the queen to retreat. } 8. Qc2 Bg4 { Further pressuring the pawn on d4. } 9. e3 { White must defend the pawn again. } 9... O-O 10. O-O Na6 11. Nc3 Qd7 { Black is well coordinated and can continue pressuring White's center. } *


[Event "Grunfeld Defense Repertoire: 3. Nf3 Variation"]
[Date "2024.05.31"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "E60"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense: Normal Variation, King's Knight Variation"]
[StudyName "Grunfeld Defense Repertoire"]
[ChapterName "3. Nf3 Variation"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/HlS091Xs/KTdczCsz"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/IndianDefense"]
[FEN "rnbqkb1r/pppppp1p/5np1/8/2PP4/8/PP2PPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 3"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2024.05.31"]
[UTCTime "12:39:23"]
[Orientation "black"]

3. Nf3 { This move is very annoying as the usual ...d5 doesn't really work. } 3... c6 { I'd prefer to avoid ...c5 and ...Bg7 which will lead into the King's Indian Defense. } (3... d5 4. cxd5 Nxd5 5. e4 { and now there's nothing to take on c3! } 5... Nb6 6. h3 Bg7 7. Nc3 { gives White a huge advantage due to the strong center. Black can't really put enough pressure on d4 and the knight on b6 can't really do anything meaningful. }) 4. Nc3 (4. g3 d5 5. Bg2 dxc4 { is my recommendation against the Catalan setup here. } 6. O-O Bg7 7. a4 Ne4 8. Qc2 Nd6 9. e4 Na6 { is fine for Black. Again, there are other lines, but this one will take you into a position that White might not be as familiar with. } { [%csl Gb4][%cal Ga6b4] }) 4... Bg7 5. e4 O-O { Inviting White to overextend in the center. } 6. Be2 (6. Bd3 d6 7. h3 (7. O-O Bg4 { is unpleasant for White }) 7... a6 8. O-O b5 9. e5 Ne8 { should be fine for Black as well. }) (6. e5 Ne8 7. Be2 d6 8. Bf4 Bg4 9. O-O Nd7 { is quite nice for Black. }) 6... d5 7. e5 Ne4 8. O-O Nxc3 9. bxc3 dxc4 10. Bxc4 c5 { This is fine for Black. } *


[Event "Grunfeld Defense Repertoire: 3. f3 Variation"]
[Date "2024.05.31"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "D70"]
[Opening "Neo-Grünfeld Defense: Goglidze Attack"]
[StudyName "Grunfeld Defense Repertoire"]
[ChapterName "3. f3 Variation"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/HlS091Xs/T1lJ97i6"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/IndianDefense"]
[FEN "rnbqkb1r/pppppp1p/5np1/8/2PP4/8/PP2PPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 3"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2024.05.31"]
[UTCTime "12:39:23"]
[Orientation "black"]

3. f3 { This is the typical Anti-Grunfeld with the idea to play e2-e4, but it's not the most challenging. Black has a ton of moves that work well. } 3... d5 { My personal favorite choice. } (3... Bg7 { leads to KID positions. }) (3... c5 4. d5 { is similar to a Benoni, but it is more favorable for Black because White can't play f4 without loss of time and can't put a knight on f3. } { [%cal Rg1f3] } 4... Bg7 5. e4 d6 { shows the typical structure. Black will play e7-e6 and counter White's pawn wedge, while White will look for e4-e5. However, without Nf3 and f4, this is much harder to achieve for White than it would be in a normal Benoni. } { [%cal Ge7e6] }) 4. cxd5 Nxd5 5. e4 Nb6 { These moves lead to Grunfeld-like positions. } 6. Nc3 Bg7 7. Be3 O-O 8. Qd2 { This might look scary, but Black is fine here. } { [%csl Gh6][%cal Ge3h6] } 8... Nc6 9. O-O-O f5 { This has been played by Carlsen and is fine for Black. } 10. e5 (10. h4 fxe4 11. h5 gxh5 12. Rxh5 Bf5 13. Rg5 Bg6 { defends successfully. The game is even. }) 10... Nb4 11. Nh3 Be6 12. Kb1 Qd7 13. Nf4 Bf7 { Black should be fine here as it's difficult for White to start a meaningful attack. For example: } { [%csl Gg4,Gf5,Gd7][%cal Rg2g4,Gd7g4] } 14. a3 N4d5 15. Ncxd5 Nxd5 16. Bc4 Nxf4 17. Bxf7+ Rxf7 18. Bxf4 e6 19. h4 h5 { This position should be okay for Black. There are of course other approaches to the 3. f3 line, but I find this one to be satisfactory. However, if you don't like this, there are always more ideas to look into. } *


